status: open

---

Implement a CLI and VS Code extension for plain-text ticket management.

Create a simple ticket-management system that operates on plain-text files.
It must preserve a workflow in which users can open and edit ticket files with
normal text editors. Git can track ticket history and resolve ID conflicts.

Ticket format

- Store tickets in a configurable directory. Use "tickets" by default.
- Use a non-negative numeric filename as the ticket ID.
- Do not require an "id" metadata field. The filename is authoritative.
- Put metadata before the first "---" separator.
- Require a status with a value of "open" or "closed".
- Treat the first non-empty line after the first separator as the title.
- Treat the remaining ticket text as its description and update history.
- Permit additional metadata fields and preserve fields that tools do not know.
- Be forgiving when parsing text written by users.
- Do not reject a ticket only because an update has an unexpected author or date format.
- Show files that cannot be interpreted as tickets in an Invalid group in the VS Code extension.

Comments and status changes

- Encourage each update to contain a date and an author name.
- Permit updates that do not follow the recommended heading format.
- Let the CLI and VS Code extension add the date and author automatically.
- If possible, get the author name and email address from the local Git configuration.
- Permit configuration to override the Git author information.
- When a tool changes status, append an update such as:

    ---

    2026-07-10 - David Reid

    Changed status to closed.

CLI

Implement a small CLI with these initial commands:

    ticket list
    ticket list --status open
    ticket list --status closed
    ticket show <id>
    ticket new
    ticket edit <id>
    ticket close <id>
    ticket reopen <id>
    ticket comment <id>

The CLI must:

- Treat the current working directory as the workspace root.
- Use a command-line option or configuration to select the ticket directory.
- Sort ticket IDs numerically.
- Use the highest existing numeric ID plus one for a new ticket.
- Let Git expose and resolve a conflict if concurrent work creates the same ID.
- Use VISUAL or EDITOR when a text editor is necessary.
- Report malformed tickets without preventing access to valid tickets where practical.
- Use safe file replacement when it changes existing tickets.
- Return a nonzero exit status when an operation fails.

VS Code extension

Implement a VS Code extension that uses the normal text editor for ticket
contents. Add a Ticket Explorer with Open, Closed, and Invalid groups.

Provide these initial commands:

- Create Ticket
- Open Ticket
- Close Ticket
- Reopen Ticket
- Add Comment
- Delete Ticket
- Refresh Tickets

The extension must:

- Treat the VS Code workspace folder as the workspace root.
- Provide a setting for the ticket directory and default it to "tickets".
- Sort ticket IDs numerically.
- Refresh when ticket files change.
- Put appropriate commands in ticket context menus.
- Ask for confirmation before deleting a ticket and use the system trash where available.
- Give useful diagnostics for files in the Invalid group.
- Parse ticket files directly so that the extension does not require a separately installed CLI program.
- Prevent concurrent extension instances from overwriting a ticket when both instances select the same new ID. The VS Code workspace file API does not provide exclusive creation through its normal write operation.

Shared behavior and tests

- Write a short specification for the ticket format and its permissive parsing rules.
- Use shared fixture files and expected results to test CLI and extension behavior.
- Include valid, unusual but accepted, and invalid ticket examples.
- Make status and filename validation strict. Keep comment parsing permissive.
- Ensure that both tools preserve user text and unknown metadata when changing a ticket.

Suggested implementation order

1. Define the format and add parser fixtures.
2. Implement CLI list and show commands.
3. Implement the remaining CLI commands.
4. Implement the VS Code Ticket Explorer.
5. Implement extension modification commands and file watching.
6. Add packaging and installation documentation.

